From: "Phil G. Fraering" <pgf@srl01.cacs.usl.edu>
Date: Wed, 11 May 1994 22:31:10 -0500
Subject: [#94-5-168] ECON: The limits to comparative advantage

Just a side comment on the post with the "there will always be something
the human programmers know that the robots won't" argument made by
John Casey.

While I currently actually think Penrose's ideas in TENM might have some
validity, I would like to point out that with programming methods like
Genetic Programming, it might be possible for a computer to "study" a
mathematical model too complicated for a human to understand.

Does anyone here remember the recent "burps" in the stock market, that
were blamed on those devilish "quants" and their computer models? There
was a full-scale slant war going on against them in the press that was
obvoious to anyone paying attention; the most prevalent deviant memoid
concerned a comparison between the maniacal, evil quantitative analysists
and the etherial scientists of Laputa engaged in studies of subjects of
absolutely no value.

Anyway, Nick Szabo and I have speculated that they're probably using
GP-like algorithms now, and modelling the economy in a partial
differential equation of (picking a number out of thin air) 150
separate variables. Noone understands this. The President (and the
rest of the government) has raised taxes and spending, which according
to him should make things fine, and can't understand why anyone would
think there's trouble ahead...

But maybe someone else, with no ideological axe to grind, sees something
in a 150-variable PDE that shows bad news for the economy in the future?

Are the computers "worried?"

