From: Alexander Chislenko <sasha@cs.umb.edu>
Date: Thu, 12 May 1994 01:00:19 -0400
Subject: [#94-5-169] ECON: Cost of social programs.

   This is an attempt to reconcile the calculations of welfare system
overhead (of 50%) and estimates of how much money actually reaches the
welfare recipients (%10 to 30%).

   There is no contradiction here, really; the difference between these
figures goes to the *third parties* that get paid more than their service
is worth on the market, or is wasted in some other way.

  Example:    My friend lives in his own house in town A;  his parents live
in a subsidized apartment in town B (there are no subsidized apts in town A,
so parents have to take a bus to visit).  Parents pay $400 for the apartment
out of $800.   The rest $400  is paid by the program;   with 50% overhead it
means that $800 is collected from [my friend and other] taxpayers.
Most likely, my friend and his wife just pay this sum themselves.
  Now here's the catch: the apartment is *not worth* $800; this figure came
out of some agreement between the program and the landlords; the same
apartments in the next building rent for $550 to $600 on the open market
(lets take $575).

  So the distribution of the $800 taxes collected for the program looks like
this:

Program overhead:	$400	(50%)
Landlord:		$225	(28%)
Recipients:		$175	(22%)

   For $1200 ($400 they pay and $800 the program costs) the parents could
easily rent a good *house* next to my friend's, or they all could get a really
big house and live there together.

   Also, the figures above do not include bus fare, telephone costs,
inconvinience to, and waste of time and effort by, both parts of the family.

  With these things added, it looks like the $800 is just stolen from my
friend's family monthly without any payback.

----------------------------------------------------------------------

   However, much of the taxes is not wasted, but just redistributed,
from some parts of the society to some other parts, which may be unfair
but may not be a complete loss.

  I would claim though that some of the redistributions are worse than waste.
  
  The market tends to redistribute wealth so that it goes from organizations
that don't know how to use it productively to those who do, and from stupid
and lazy people to those smart and creative, which, among other things, means
that investments become more efficient and consumption stimulates further
growth (tax paying successful professionals buy more high-tech gadgets, 
museum tickets and college courses, and spend less on baseball than tax
collecting bureaucrats and welfare recipients).
   Welfare seems to do exactly the opposite thing.
I wonder how much money that we could direct to ExI went into sports, beer,
support of atavistic ideologies, etc.

